{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>You have begun to show Your servant, etc.</b> Rashi explained: He opened to stand and pray. The peh of פָּתַח is vocalized with a segol, and this is its meaning: הַחִלּוֹתָ לְהַרְאוֹת — what did He show him? An opening (פֶּתַח), like one who opens an opening to some thing. And afterwards he explained for what purpose He showed him an opening — to stand and pray. But those who read פָּתַח, with the peh vocalized with a qamatz, it is an error at the hands of scribes, for what does this come to teach us? For surely he did not conclude his prayer before he opened. Why did he use the expression ״פָּתַח״? He should have said ״he stood and prayed״." "And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Berakhot 32a): A person should always arrange the praise of the Place [i.e., HaShem] before he prays, for so Moses did — he began with the praise of the Place, as it says: ״אַתָּה הַחִלּוֹתָ לְהַרְאוֹת אֶת עַבְדְּךָ״ and so forth, and afterwards he said: ״אֶעְבְּרָה נָּא״. The explanation of the matter is that every righteous person seeks a free gift, and reckons within himself that all that the Holy One, blessed be He, does with him is חֶסֶד אֵל כָּל הַיּוֹם, for the Holy One, blessed be He, is always the one who begins in the bestowal of good upon a person from the day of his birth, and all the good deeds a person accomplishes — it is all by way of repayment, and the Holy One, blessed be He, is the great Creditor who surrounds and comes round again and gives, in a surrounding [surfeit], to the one who repaid a first debt — as it is written (Job 41:3): ״מִי הִקְדִּימַנִי וַאֲשַׁלֵּם״ — this teaches that the Holy One, blessed be He, is the one who goes first and begins in all good things. And this is the praise and glory of the Place, blessed be He, that the one who prays must arrange His praise first, before he prays, so that throughout the entire prayer and supplications he asks nothing but a free gift — for so we find in Moses: before he said ״אֶעְבְּרָה נָּא״, he said ״אַתָּה הַחִלּוֹתָ״ and so forth, teaching that the Holy One, blessed be He, is the one who begins in all good things, and [Moses] has no debt against Him, and he sought a free gift. And this is what Rashi explained: ״אֶת גׇּדְלְךָ״ — this is the attribute of Your goodness; ״וְאֶת יָדְךָ הַחֲזָקָה״ — that overpowers the attribute of judgment, for according to the line of judgment He owes him nothing at all, and in the abundance of His goodness He bestows kindness upon one who has nothing against Him. And this is a precious interpretation."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The Kli Yakar quotes Rashi's comment on the word פתח; I have preserved Rashi's own wordplay in English as best as possible. The segol vs. qamatz distinction is between פֶּתַח (noun: 'an opening') and פָּתַח (verb: 'he opened')."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:11:16.541916341Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}